{
  "requirement": "`Sources::search_structure` returns the enclosing outline addresses and matched\nexcerpts in `matches`. The [`SearchResults`](#habeas-req-requirements.sdk.graph.search.SearchResults)\nfield `complete` has the same meaning as for a text search.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub type StructuralMatches = SearchResults<StructuralMatch>;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations from `requirements.sdk.graph.search.StructuralMatches`**\n1. `Sources::search_structure` returns the enclosing outline address of each match in `matches`.\n2. It returns the matched excerpt for each match.\n3. `complete` means what it means for a text search: whether the result includes every match in scope. It is true when nothing is omitted and false when a match is omitted.\n4. `StructuralMatches` is the type alias `SearchResults<StructuralMatch>`.\n\n**Test coverage**\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/source_defaults.rs#fn[built_in_languages_and_structural_search_use_the_project_scope]` runs the real `Sources::search_structure` against a temp project.\n  - **Address and excerpt:** The single match has `node.path == visible.rs`, `node.selector == \"fn[visible]\"` and `excerpt == \"call()\"`. This shows the enclosing outline address and the excerpt.\n  - **Unbounded search:** The result is `complete` with one match.\n  - **Limit 0:** One match exists in scope, none are returned, and `complete` is false.\n  - **Limit equal to the match count:** Limit 1 with one match is complete.\n  - **Limit below the match count:** After a second Rust file is added, an unbounded search gives two matches and is complete. A limit of 1 gives one match and `!complete`. This is the key omitted-match case.\n  - **Empty scopes:** Scopes that hold no matches (ignored files, `skip`, `.git`) give empty results that are `complete`.\n  - **Language and scope filtering:** Python search returns only `script.py`. A scope of `visible.rs` returns that file's match. An out-of-scope `..` returns an error.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/tests.rs#fn[structural_search_uses_overridden_parses_their_snapshot_and_requested_scope]` runs the provider's default `search_structure` through a custom parse provider. It checks the same limit cases: `None` and `Some(1)` give one complete match, and `Some(0)` gives zero matches and `complete == false`. It also checks the path, selector and excerpt of the match.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` checks the public export inventory, which includes the `StructuralMatches` alias. The alias is a declaration, so checking it through the public API inventory is adequate.\n\n**Gaps considered**\n- The `complete` semantics are the same as for text search. `SearchResults` has its own tests, including `completeness_is_false_only_when_a_match_is_omitted_and_errors_propagate`, and this requirement only has to show the structural path obeys them. The tests above show that.\n- I found no untested obligation and no counterexample that these assertions would accept. The assertions check actual values, not just execution.\n\nNo acknowledgments (`{fixme}`) apply or are needed.",
  "references": "14649e5e3ebcf0a600161bf0c726caca908cf819a48e504b1f32aba141f0539e",
